# encoder_alloc_scratch_2026-08-02 — provenance
# Companion to encoder_alloc_scratch_2026-08-02.md and its two TSVs.
#
# This is a CHANGE + its before/after measurement, not a profiling session:
# levers 3a and 3 of benchmarks/encoder_hotspot_reprofile_2026-08-02.md
# ("tier the forward-pass scratch", +5.30 ms projected; "per-txb scratch reuse",
# +24.76 ms projected). The re-profile's own ceilings are ARITHMETIC ON MEASURED
# SELF-COSTS, not forecasts of a change; this file is what the change actually
# returned.

date                 2026-08-02
box                  Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, macOS 26.5.2 (Darwin arm64)
baseline commit      578653ff30b4116e29453b414c4da0702959ed63  (the re-profile)
worktree             zenav1-aom--alloc, detached at main
libaom oracle        upstream/ submodule 03087864cf4bea6abb0d28f95cf7843511413d8f (v3.14.1),
                     prebuilt libaom.a copied in from the primary checkout (same submodule sha)
rustc                1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
C compiler           Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101)
build flags          --release, NO -C target-cpu=native (runtime SIMD dispatch, what users get)
threading            single-threaded on both arms
nice                 every measured run under `nice -n 19`

## BOX WAS NOT IDLE — read this before reading any millisecond

Whole-box load average 28-34 (of 12 cores) throughout, the same concurrent
`zenmetrics` fleet the re-profile ran under. Handled the same way, and the
reason for the new harness below:

* scripts/eprof_ab.sh — an INTERLEAVED control band over N arbitrary encoder
  binaries (eprof_control.sh only interleaves two fixed arms). One invocation of
  every arm per round, round after round, so drift lands on all arms equally.
  Each invocation is 2 warm-up + 7 timed encodes with its own median, identical
  to eprof_control.sh. Comparing separately-taken medians is exactly what
  docs/DIFFERENTIAL_PLAYBOOK.md §6 forbids on this box.
* scripts/eprof_ab_stats.py — per-arm median/min/max/spread FIRST, then the
  paired per-round ratios. The paired-median column is the load-robust one.

## Profile cell — unchanged from both encoder profiles, for continuity

1024x1024 photo, cq-level 44, cpu-used 6, ALLINTRA (usage 2), 8-bit 4:2:0,
single tile, single thread. Source $HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv (the file
the 2026-08-01 cross-encoder study and both profiles used). Both arms emit the
same 4472-byte stream at this cell.

## Exact commands

  git worktree add --detach ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--alloc main
  git -C ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--alloc submodule update --init
  cp -R ../zenav1-aom/upstream/build upstream/build          # same submodule sha
  cp -R ../zenav1-aom/conformance/data conformance/data      # gitignored corpus

  nice -n 19 cc -O2 -o benchmarks/xbench/target/drv_libaom \
      benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c -Iupstream -Iupstream/build \
      -Lupstream/build -laom -lm -lc++
  (cd benchmarks/xbench && nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p zenav1-aom-bench --example eprof_alloc

  # one drv-aom per arm, kept side by side in ~/tmp/allocbin/
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_ab.sh 12 <out.tsv> \
      base=...BASE l3a=...L3a l3=...L3ONLY final=...FINAL \
      libaom-c=benchmarks/xbench/target/drv_libaom
  python3 scripts/eprof_ab_stats.py <out.tsv> --ref libaom-c --vs base

  nice -n 19 ./target/release/examples/eprof_alloc 1024 1024 44 6 \
      ~/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv

## Byte-identity gate

  cargo nextest run --cargo-profile test-fast --workspace --no-fail-fast \
      --run-ignored all                       # SIMD dispatch live
  AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1 cargo nextest run --cargo-profile test-fast --workspace \
      --no-fail-fast --run-ignored all        # scalar-pinned
  cargo check --target x86_64-apple-darwin --workspace --all-targets

`--run-ignored all` is included deliberately: the KB-PERF-1 session found two
stale pins that way. The conformance corpus must be present
(`conformance/data`, gitignored) or ~40 tests fail-loud with "conformance
vector missing" — that is the fail-loud design working, not a regression.

## Committed data files

  .ab.tsv         the interleaved control band, every arm x every round
  .alloc.tsv      the allocation census, per arm

## Known gaps

* ONE cell, one image, one content class. cpu-used 3/4/5/9 not measured here —
  the re-profile records that 9 (5.64x) and 4 (7.76x) are worse cells and were
  never decomposed.
* Wall-clock only. No instruction count (no valgrind on Apple Silicon), so
  "fewer allocator calls" and "less time in the allocator" are two different
  measurements and only the second one is what the ratio moved on.
* No multi-threaded measurement; neither arm is threaded.
